module default_named.neg_2

// ONE seeded cause: a parameter WITHOUT a default stands after one WITH.
//
// D102 declaration-rule 1. It is a rule about the ORDER, so no single parameter
// is wrong on its own -- which is why it is judged of the declaration and named
// there. Without the rule, `f(x int = 1, y int)` would have no legal call at
// all: `y` could only be reached by name, and D102 call-rule 3 forbids a
// positional argument after a named one, so `x` could never be passed
// positionally either.
//
// AGREEMENT, measured 2026-08-23: the oracle refuses it too, naming `y` and the
// same rule (its wording is Russian; the outcome is what the pair asserts).
fn bump(x int = 1, y int) -> int => x + y

fn main() {
    println(bump(y: 2))
}
